{-
   A parallel copy whose cycle swaps values of two widths - the case a 32-bit `xchg` destroys.

   `relay` below receives `(unit, sink)` in the argument registers and calls something that takes
   `(sink, unit)`, so the two values have to trade places. Both ends are registers and the general
   file has an exchange instruction, so the allocator breaks the cycle with one `xchg` rather than
   going through a scratch register - and a `RegMove` names one class where an exchange moves two
   values.

   The narrow one decided the width, and `xchg r32, r32` *zeroes* the upper half of both registers:
   the sink arrived truncated to its low word and the first write through it faulted. The exchange is
   encoded at the wider of the two ends now - see sequenceMoves in codegen/x64/legalize.cpp.

   **What makes this a fixture rather than a note:** nothing about it is unusual. Two arguments in
   the other order is the most ordinary call there is, and it only reaches an exchange when the
   register allocator has put both values where the other one is going - which is what an argument
   pair does at every call whose parameters are declared the other way round.
-}

fn write(&to: String, unit: Int) -> {} = String.pushUnit(to, unit)

-- The swap: `(Int, &String)` in, `(&String, Int)` out.
fn relay(unit: Int, &to: String) -> {} = write(to, unit)

-- The same trade with the pointer arriving first, so the cycle is broken from the other side.
fn relayBack(&to: String, unit: Int) -> {} = writeOther(unit, to)

fn writeOther(unit: Int, &to: String) -> {} = String.pushUnit(to, unit)

-- Three values, so the cycle is longer than a pair and the exchange is not the whole of it.
fn rotate(a: Int, b: Int, &to: String) -> {} = takeThree(to, b, a)

fn takeThree(&to: String, first: Int, second: Int) -> {}:
    String.pushUnit(to, first)
    String.pushUnit(to, second)

fn main() -> Int:
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(16 :: Size)

    relay(65, out)          -- `A`
    relayBack(out, 66)      -- `B`
    rotate(67, 68, out)     -- `DC`

    let &total = 0
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(out):
        total = total + String.unit(out, i)
        i = i + 1

    -- 65 + 66 + 68 + 67, which is every unit written and in a sum that changes if any of them is
    -- wrong. The text is four units long, which the length below asserts separately.
    return total + (truncate(length(out)) :: Int)
